# Faris-Lavine comparison operator N and commutation condition for the *auxiliary positive
# sum-of-squares operator* of the Navier-Stokes sector.
#
# NAME NOTE (2026-09-19).  The operator H below is NOT the Navier-Stokes Hamiltonian.  It is
# the positive Weyl-ordered sum of squares of the constraint forms - an *auxiliary positive
# operator* for the NS sector, and it is what the Faris-Lavine comparison `N` is built from.
#
# CAUTION on book.tex's "H^2(x)".  The paragraph after book.tex eq. 4186 proposes "using
# H^2(x) as a positive auxiliary operator in Corollary 1.1".  That literal object - the SQUARE
# of the generator - is NOT a valid Faris-Lavine comparison N in general, and must not be
# confused with the sum-of-squares operator used here.  Two independent reasons, the first
# formalized in the tree:
#   (i) the criterion (BookProof.ChapterFarisLavineCore.essentiallySelfAdjointOn_of_farisLavine)
#       requires `N + 1` ONTO, and range(H^2 + 1) is contained in range(H - i) because
#       H^2 + 1 = (H - i)(H + i) on the domain; so H^2 + 1 is not onto whenever H has a
#       non-trivial deficiency - exactly the situation of the formalized refutation
#       BookProof.FarisLavine.not_farisLavine_criterion_of_relative_bound (the limit-circle
#       Jacobi operator with N = H satisfies both inequalities while ESA fails);
#   (ii) D(H^2) is strictly smaller than D(H), so H^2 cannot serve as a comparison on the
#       common FL domain at all.
# The valid comparison for the NS *Hamiltonian* leg is instead the Leray energy
# N_E = 1 + |u|^2 (BookProof.NsKoopman.nsEnergyOp, commForm_kvn_energy_bound,
# nsKoopman_esa_of_energy_comparison); see `docs/ns_kvn_equation.cdb` CHECK 4-6.
# The Navier-Stokes Hamiltonian proper is the Koopman-von Neumann / Liouville generator of the
# FULL residual, which carries the pressure gradient q_i = d_i p (and the external force f_i) as
# well as the advection and the viscosity,
#     H_NS = pi^i ( u_j u_{i,j} + q_i - nu u_{i,jj} ) + h.c.
# (tree: the residual is `BookProof.NsFullEuler.nsResPoly` = u_j u_{i,j} + q_i - nu w_i, with the
# incompressibility `divPoly` = sum_j u_{j,j} = 0; in the mainstream Leray-Galerkin form the
# pressure is eliminated by the Leray projection and the drift reduces to
# `BookProof.NsKoopman.kvnPoly` / `nsKoopmanOp`).  It is *not* bounded below and it reproduces the
# Navier-Stokes equations through i[H_NS, u_k] = 2 F_k,
# F_k = u_j u_{k,j} + q_k - nu u_{k,jj} + f_k.  That statement - the one that identifies the NS
# Hamiltonian - is verified in `docs/ns_kvn_equation.cdb` CHECK 1 (commutator) and CHECK 8
# (pressure/constraint); this module verifies only the comparison-side algebra for the auxiliary
# operator.
#
# MOTIVATION. The essential self-adjointness of the field-space Hamiltonians is proved in
# `../timepiece` through the commutator criterion of
#     W. G. Faris and R. B. Lavine, Commutators and self-adjointness of Hamiltonian
#     operators, Commun. Math. Phys. 35 (1974), 39-48, Theorem 1,
# formalized in BookProof/ChapterFarisLavineCore.essentiallySelfAdjointOn_of_farisLavine.
# Theorem 1 asks for a positive self-adjoint operator N whose domain carries H (so H is
# defined on D(N)), with N + 1 onto, and the commutator-form bound
#     ± i [H, N]  <=  c N        i.e.  |commForm H N u| <= c * quadForm N u
# on the core.  This module certifies the SYMBOLIC content of that certificate for the
# auxiliary positive operator of the Navier-Stokes sector (NOT the NS Hamiltonian - see the
# NAME NOTE above)
#     H  =  (1/2) sum_j kappa_j pi_j^2  +  (1/2) sum_r L_r^2 ,   L_r = sum_i v_{ri} x_i ,
#     pi_j = -i d/dx_j ,
# against the harmonic comparison operator
#     N  =  -Delta + |x|^2 / 4  =  sum_j ( -d_j^2 + (1/4) x_j^2 ) .
# This is exactly the Faris-Lavine certificate of
#     BookProof.ChapterSqSumFarisLavine      (commPoly_eq, commConst, commForm_sqSumOp_le,
#                                             norm_sqSumOp_le)
# consumed by BookProof.FarisLavineOnly.sqSumOp_esa_farisLavine and by every gauge-fixed NS
# parcel family (BookProof.NsOuterFock.nsOuterFock_esa_farisLavine, c = 1/2 + 84 B^2).
#
# WHAT A .cdb MODULE CAN AND CANNOT CERTIFY.  Cadabra is a symbolic/algebraic engine on
# classical expressions.  It certifies here: (i) that N is the positive harmonic
# comparison (a sum of squares); (ii) that the commutator [H, N] is FIRST ORDER and has
# exactly the shape the Lean commutator lemma claims - a constant plus symmetrized
# momentum-coordinate products, the potential entering only through its gradient - so that
# the commutator-form bound is a statement about the one-particle symbol; (iii) the two
# elementary dominance identities (AM-GM and Young) that turn that shape into the bound
# with the constants c = km/2 + 2M and K = 3 km/2 + 8 B.  It does NOT certify the Hilbert
# space statements (symmetry, positivity as a quadratic form, N + 1 onto, the closedness of
# the core); those are proved in Lean.
#
# PIPELINE.  Cells below:
#   N               - the comparison operator, the sum of squares -d_j^2 + (1/4) x_j^2.
#   Vpot            - the potential V = (1/2) sum_r L_r^2 (the second half of H).
#   comm            - [H, N] phi computed by the Leibniz rule (product_rule), constants
#                     differentiated away; must be first order in phi.
#   comm_exp        - the claimed shape (commPoly_eq): a constant plus 2 (grad_j V) d_j
#                     and the symmetrized momentum-coordinate products.
#   check_comm      - comm - comm_exp, which must vanish identically.
#   G1, G2          - the gradient d_j V (must equal grad_j = sum_r v_{rj} L_r).
#   H11, H22        - the Laplacian d_j^2 V (must equal sum_r v_{rj}^2); these two are the
#                     entries of the constant commConst.
#   commConst       - -(1/4) sum_j kappa_j + sum_r sum_k v_{rk}^2  (the Lean commConst).
#   amgm, young     - the dominance identities (a b <= a^2 + b^2/4) and
#                     (2 g a <= g^2/(2M) + 2M a^2) as polynomial identities.
#
# Run: cadabra2-cli -q -n <this file>

def expand_derivs(ex, n):
	"""Leibniz expansion of every nested partial derivative."""
	for i in range(n):
		product_rule(ex)
		distribute(ex)
	collect_terms(ex)

\partial{#}::PartialDerivative.
{x1,x2}::Depends(\partial{#}).
V::Depends(\partial{#}).
\phi::Depends(\partial{#}).

# ===== derivative rules: coordinate derivatives (d_j x_k = delta_{jk}) and the
# differentiation-away of the constant coefficients (kappa, v).  Order matters: the
# delta rules for the coordinates must precede any "= 0" rule for the same pattern.
xkills = [r"\partial_{1}{x1} -> 1", r"\partial_{2}{x2} -> 1",
          r"\partial_{1}{x2} -> 0", r"\partial_{2}{x1} -> 0",
          r"\partial_{1 1}{x1} -> 0", r"\partial_{2 2}{x1} -> 0", r"\partial_{1 2}{x1} -> 0",
          r"\partial_{1 1}{x2} -> 0", r"\partial_{2 2}{x2} -> 0", r"\partial_{1 2}{x2} -> 0"]
ckills = []
for c in ["k1","k2","v11","v12","v21","v22"]:
	for d in ["1","2","1 1","2 2","1 2"]:
		ckills.append(r"\partial_{%s}{%s} -> 0" % (d, c))
const_rules = Ex(", ".join(xkills + ckills))

# ===== 1. the comparison operator N = -Delta + |x|^2/4 = sum_j ( -d_j^2 + (1/4)x_j^2 )
# (D = 2 here; no step below uses the dimension, so the general case is the same term by
#  term).  Each summand's principal symbol is p_j^2 + x_j^2/4 >= 0: N is a positive sum of
#  squares, which is the positivity hypothesis of Theorem 1.
Npot := (1/4)(x1 x1 + x2 x2);

# ===== 2. the Hamiltonian: H = -(1/2)(k1 d1^2 + k2 d2^2) + Vpot, Vpot = (1/2) sum_r L_r^2
L1 := v11 x1 + v12 x2.
L2 := v21 x1 + v22 x2.
Vpot := (1/2)( @(L1) @(L1) + @(L2) @(L2) );

# ===== 3. the commutator [H, N] = H(N phi) - N(H phi), expanded by the Leibniz rule =====
Nphi := -\partial_{1}{\partial_{1}{\phi}} - \partial_{2}{\partial_{2}{\phi}} + @(Npot) \phi.
Hphi := -(1/2) k1 \partial_{1}{\partial_{1}{\phi}} - (1/2) k2 \partial_{2}{\partial_{2}{\phi}} + V \phi.
comm := -(1/2) k1 \partial_{1}{\partial_{1}{ @(Nphi) }} - (1/2) k2 \partial_{2}{\partial_{2}{ @(Nphi) }} + V @(Nphi)
        + \partial_{1}{\partial_{1}{ @(Hphi) }} + \partial_{2}{\partial_{2}{ @(Hphi) }} - @(Npot) @(Hphi);
expand_derivs(comm, 12)
substitute(comm, const_rules, repeat=True)
sort_product(comm); canonicalise(comm); collect_terms(comm)

# The claimed shape (BookProof.ChapterSqSumFarisLavine.commPoly_eq, read in the standard
# pi_j = -i d_j convention): a constant, the symmetrized -kappa_j/2 (x_j d_j) products, and
# twice the gradient of the potential dotted into the derivatives.
comm_exp := -(1/4)(k1 + k2) \phi
            - (1/2) k1 x1 \partial_{1}{\phi} - (1/2) k2 x2 \partial_{2}{\phi}
            + 2 \partial_{1}{V} \partial_{1}{\phi} + 2 \partial_{2}{V} \partial_{2}{\phi}
            + \partial_{1 1}{V} \phi + \partial_{2 2}{V} \phi;
check_comm := @(comm) - @(comm_exp);
distribute(check_comm); sort_product(check_comm); canonicalise(check_comm); collect_terms(check_comm)
print("CHECK 1  NS  commutator [H,N] has the Faris-Lavine shape (0 = ok) :", check_comm)

# ===== 4. the potential data: d_j V and d_j^2 V for V = (1/2) sum_r L_r^2 =====
# d_j V must equal grad_j = sum_r v_{rj} L_r   (BookProof.ChapterSqSumFarisLavine.gradPoly),
# and d_j^2 V must equal sum_r v_{rj}^2, whose sum is the constant commConst.
G1 := \partial_{1}{ @(Vpot) }.
G2 := \partial_{2}{ @(Vpot) }.
for ex in [G1, G2]:
	expand_derivs(ex, 6)
	substitute(ex, const_rules, repeat=True)
	sort_product(ex); canonicalise(ex); collect_terms(ex)
grad1 := v11 @(L1) + v21 @(L2).
grad2 := v12 @(L1) + v22 @(L2).
check_g1 := @(G1) - @(grad1);
check_g2 := @(G2) - @(grad2);
distribute(check_g1); sort_product(check_g1); canonicalise(check_g1); collect_terms(check_g1)
distribute(check_g2); sort_product(check_g2); canonicalise(check_g2); collect_terms(check_g2)
print("CHECK 2a NS  d1 V = grad_1                        (0 = ok) :", check_g1)
print("CHECK 2b NS  d2 V = grad_2                        (0 = ok) :", check_g2)

H11 := \partial_{1}{ \partial_{1}{ @(Vpot) } }.
H22 := \partial_{2}{ \partial_{2}{ @(Vpot) } }.
for ex in [H11, H22]:
	expand_derivs(ex, 8)
	substitute(ex, const_rules, repeat=True)
	sort_product(ex); canonicalise(ex); collect_terms(ex)
check_h11 := @(H11) - (v11 v11 + v21 v21);
check_h22 := @(H22) - (v12 v12 + v22 v22);
distribute(check_h11); collect_terms(check_h11)
distribute(check_h22); collect_terms(check_h22)
print("CHECK 2c NS  d1^2 V = sum_r (v_r1)^2             (0 = ok) :", check_h11)
print("CHECK 2d NS  d2^2 V = sum_r (v_r2)^2             (0 = ok) :", check_h22)

# The constant of commPoly_eq: -(1/4) sum_j kappa_j + sum_r sum_k v_{rk}^2.
commConst := -(1/4)(k1 + k2) + (v11 v11 + v12 v12 + v21 v21 + v22 v22);
print("CHECK 3  NS  commConst = -1/4 sum kappa + sum v^2        :", commConst)

# ===== 5. the two dominance identities behind the bound =====
# The commutator contributes, per mode, |kappa_j|/2 * (b_j a_j) and, from the gradient,
# 2 g_j a_j, with a_j = ||d_j u||, b_j = ||x_j u||, g_j = ||(grad_j) u||.  The bound
#     |commForm| <= (km/2 + 2M) quadForm N,   quadForm N = sum_j (a_j^2 + b_j^2/4),
# follows from the two elementary identities below, together with the Schur bound
# sum_j g_j^2 <= M^2 sum_j b_j^2 (BookProof.ChapterSqSumFarisLavine.sum_gradFun_sq_le_of_schur).
{a,b,g,M}::Commuting.
amgm := (a a + (1/4) b b) - a b;
amgm_fact := (a - b/2) (a - b/2);
check_amgm := @(amgm) - @(amgm_fact);
distribute(check_amgm); sort_product(check_amgm); canonicalise(check_amgm); collect_terms(check_amgm)
print("CHECK 4a NS  a^2 + b^2/4 - a b = (a - b/2)^2       (0 = ok) :", check_amgm)

# Multiplied through by 2M to keep the check polynomial:
#   2M (g^2/(2M) + 2M a^2 - 2 g a) = g^2 + 4 M^2 a^2 - 4 M g a = (g - 2 M a)^2.
young := (g g) + 4 M M (a a) - 4 M g a;
young_fact := (g - 2 M a) (g - 2 M a);
check_young := @(young) - @(young_fact);
distribute(check_young); sort_product(check_young); canonicalise(check_young); collect_terms(check_young)
print("CHECK 4b NS  g^2/(2M) + 2M a^2 - 2 g a = (g-2Ma)^2/(2M) (0 = ok) :", check_young)

# ============================================================================
# PART B - THE CORE, AND THE LIFT TO THE OUTER FOCK SPACE (2026-09-21)
# ============================================================================
# What Part A above does NOT certify, and what the plan now states explicitly (§D6b):
# Faris-Lavine is run on a CORE C_0, and the criterion (`CoreData`) needs the comparison N
# ITSELF to be symmetric on C_0 and C_0 to be a core for N (dense in the GRAPH NORM of N).
# For the oscillator comparison N1 = -Delta + |x|^2/4 the concrete core is the Hermite /
# Gauss-polynomial core `polyGaussCore D`, and that N1 is ESA on it is a theorem, not a
# formality.  Part B certifies the symbolic content of that theorem, and the fact that the
# outer comparison is a DIFFERENT operator (`dGamma(N)`), so the ESA must be reproved on the
# LIFTED core.

# ===== 5. the CORE: N is an oscillator, hence ESA on the Hermite core =====
# With a = d/dx + x/2 and adag = -d/dx + x/2 (one degree; D degrees are the sum):
#   (a) FACTORIZATION  adag a = -d^2 + x^2/4 - 1/2, i.e. H_osc = adag a + 1/2 >= 1/2;
#   (b) CCR             [a, adag] = 1;
#   (c) LADDER          [adag a, a] = -a ,  [adag a, adag] = adag.
# (a)+(b) exhibit the ground state (a phi_0 = 0, phi_0 = e^{-x^2/4}); (b)+(c) make the
# Hermite functions adag^n phi_0 the eigenvectors of H_osc with eigenvalues n + 1/2, ARITHMETIC
# in n.  On that core H_osc acts diagonally with linear growth, so every core vector is an
# analytic (Nelson) vector for H_osc and the core is dense in its graph norm: H_osc is
# essentially self-adjoint on the Hermite core.  This is obligation (i) at the symbolic level;
# the Lean statements are `harmonicOsc_essentiallySelfAdjoint` /
# `oscillator_essentiallySelfAdjoint_on_hermiteCore`.
apsi := \partial_{1}{\phi} + (x1/2) \phi;
adagpsi := -\partial_{1}{\phi} + (x1/2) \phi;
hoscpsi := -\partial_{1}{\partial_{1}{\phi}} + (x1 x1/4) \phi;
adagapsi := -\partial_{1}{ @(apsi) } + (x1/2) @(apsi);
aadagpsi := \partial_{1}{ @(adagpsi) } + (x1/2) @(adagpsi);
for ex in [adagapsi, aadagpsi]:
	expand_derivs(ex, 6)
	substitute(ex, const_rules, repeat=True)
	sort_product(ex); canonicalise(ex); collect_terms(ex)
osc_check := @(adagapsi) - (@(hoscpsi) - (1/2) \phi);
distribute(osc_check); sort_product(osc_check); canonicalise(osc_check); collect_terms(osc_check)
print("CHECK 5a NS  adag a = H_osc - 1/2                       (0 = ok) :", osc_check)
ccr_check := @(aadagpsi) - @(adagapsi) - \phi;
distribute(ccr_check); collect_terms(ccr_check)
print("CHECK 5b NS  [a, adag] = 1                              (0 = ok) :", ccr_check)
# The ladder follows from the CCR alone: [adag a, a] = adag [a,a] + [adag,a] a = -a.
{\aop, \adagop}::NonCommuting.
ladder_d := \adagop \aop \aop - \aop \adagop \aop + \aop;
substitute(ladder_d, $ \aop \adagop -> 1 + \adagop \aop $, repeat=True);
distribute(ladder_d); collect_terms(ladder_d)
print("CHECK 5c NS  [adag a, a] = -a                           (0 = ok) :", ladder_d)
ladder_u := \adagop \aop \adagop - \adagop \adagop \aop - \adagop;
substitute(ladder_u, $ \aop \adagop -> 1 + \adagop \aop $, repeat=True);
distribute(ladder_u); collect_terms(ladder_u)
print("CHECK 5d NS  [adag a, adag] = adag                      (0 = ok) :", ladder_u)

# ===== 6. the LIFT: the outer comparison is dGamma(N), and ESA must be reproved there =====
# The outer comparison is NOT N: it is the finite-particle / sector lift
#     N_out = dsOp (fun n => N1 + ... + N1) = dGamma(N1)
# on the nested Fock space, and the lifted Hamiltonian is dGamma(H1) (or the parcel sum), not H1.
# The one-particle statement "N1 is ESA on C_0" does NOT imply "dGamma(H1) is ESA on the lifted
# core": the lifted core is the finite-particle TENSOR core built from C_0, and the operator is
# a different one.  What the symbolic algebra certifies is that dGamma(N) acts
# ONE-PARTICLE-WISE (the second-quantization identity)
#     dGamma(N) = sum_{i,j} N_ij adag_i a_j ,
#     [dGamma(N), adag_k] = sum_i N_ik adag_i ,   [dGamma(N), a_k] = - sum_i N_ki a_i ,
# which is exactly what makes the lifted core the tensor core and what a core-transfer proof
# must consume.  The Lean statements for obligation (ii) are
# `dGamma_essentiallySelfAdjointOn_fockCore` / `dGamma_essentiallySelfAdjointOn_of_esa` and the
# sector-wise `outerHam_esa_fl` / `dsOp_essentiallySelfAdjointOn`.
{\a{1}, \a{2}, \adag{1}, \adag{2}}::NonCommuting.
{N11op, N12op, N21op, N22op}::Commuting.
DG := N11op \adag{1} \a{1} + N12op \adag{1} \a{2} + N21op \adag{2} \a{1} + N22op \adag{2} \a{2};
liftcre := @(DG) \adag{2} - \adag{2} @(DG);
distribute(liftcre); collect_terms(liftcre);
substitute(liftcre, $ \a{1} \adag{1} -> 1 + \adag{1} \a{1} $, repeat=True);
substitute(liftcre, $ \a{2} \adag{2} -> 1 + \adag{2} \a{2} $, repeat=True);
substitute(liftcre, $ \a{1} \adag{2} -> \adag{2} \a{1} $, repeat=True);
substitute(liftcre, $ \a{2} \adag{1} -> \adag{1} \a{2} $, repeat=True);
substitute(liftcre, $ \adag{2} \adag{1} -> \adag{1} \adag{2} $, repeat=True);
sort_product(liftcre); canonicalise(liftcre); collect_terms(liftcre);
liftcrecheck := @(liftcre) - (N12op \adag{1} + N22op \adag{2});
distribute(liftcrecheck); collect_terms(liftcrecheck)
print("CHECK 6a NS  [dGamma N, adag_2] = N_12 adag_1 + N_22 adag_2  (0 = ok) :", liftcrecheck)
liftann := @(DG) \a{2} - \a{2} @(DG);
distribute(liftann); collect_terms(liftann);
for _step in range(6):
	substitute(liftann, $ \a{2} \adag{1} -> \adag{1} \a{2} $, repeat=True);
	substitute(liftann, $ \a{2} \adag{2} -> 1 + \adag{2} \a{2} $, repeat=True);
	substitute(liftann, $ \a{2} \a{1} -> \a{1} \a{2} $, repeat=True);
	distribute(liftann); collect_terms(liftann);
sort_product(liftann); canonicalise(liftann); collect_terms(liftann);
liftanncheck := @(liftann) - (-N21op \a{1} - N22op \a{2});
distribute(liftanncheck); collect_terms(liftanncheck)
print("CHECK 6b NS  [dGamma N, a_2] = -(N_21 a_1 + N_22 a_2)         (0 = ok) :", liftanncheck)
